/// \class MultiplayerSettings
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_ini_definition_parsers
/// \brief The multiplayer lobby's settings and player colour list, filled
///        by the MultiplayerSettings and MultiplayerColor INI blocks.
///
/// BFME 2 keeps one instance, TheMultiplayerSettings. It is an engine
/// subsystem: GameEngine::init registers it through
/// SubsystemInterfaceList::initSubsystem, and INI::parseMultiplayerSettingsDefinition
/// creates one itself if the pointer is still null when the first
/// MultiplayerSettings block is read. The class is not Zero Hour's. BFME 2's
/// MultiplayerSettings block has five starting-credit preset fields and its
/// colour definition has extra fields, neither of which is in Zero Hour's
/// MultiplayerSettings and MultiplayerColor field tables, and the object has
/// a different size from Zero Hour's (the BFME 1 donor sources note the same
/// for BFME 1). Zero Hour does have starting-money presets, by another
/// route (see "What it holds").
///
/// \par What it holds
/// - Scalar settings, set from the MultiplayerSettings block:
///   StartCountdownTimer, MaxBeaconsPerPlayer, the four switches UseShroud,
///   ShowRandomPlayerTemplate, ShowRandomStartPos and ShowRandomColor, and
///   five starting-credit presets, InitialCreditsVeryLow, InitialCreditsLow,
///   InitialCreditsMedium, InitialCreditsHigh and InitialCreditsVeryHigh.
///   Zero Hour's MultiplayerSettings block has the first six of these only.
///   Zero Hour supplies starting-money choices through separate
///   MultiplayerStartingMoneyChoice blocks instead (Zero Hour:
///   INIMultiplayer.cpp), each with a Value and a Default flag, which feed a
///   list with one default choice. BFME 2's block carries the five fixed
///   credit-preset fields instead, and the recovered block-keyword list
///   (`reverse/ini_blocks.tsv`) has no MultiplayerStartingMoneyChoice entry.
///   That list keeps only keyword registrations that could be paired
///   unambiguously, so it does not by itself prove the retail loader lacks
///   the keyword, and whether BFME 2 also keeps a starting-money list in
///   code is not established here.
/// - A list of player colours: an ordered map from a whole-number index to a
///   colour definition, plus a cached count that is refilled from the map's
///   size whenever it reads zero. A colour definition holds a tooltip name,
///   an RGB colour and the same colour packed into one 32-bit value, a
///   second pair for the night variant, two further RGB colours and one
///   flag. Zero Hour's definition has only the tooltip name and the two
///   colour pairs.
/// - Two further colour definitions that are not in the list, one for the
///   observer and one for the random choice. No INI block or field parsed
///   by the functions in this group reaches them.
///
/// \par Defaults
/// The constructor sets, before any INI is read: a limit of 3 beacons per
/// player, all four switches on, credit presets of 500, 1000, 1500, 2000 and
/// 2500 from VeryLow to VeryHigh, and zero for StartCountdownTimer (as in
/// Zero Hour). It also stores 10000 in a member just before the
/// StartCountdownTimer field that no field of the block writes; the
/// constructor's own view calls that member the countdown timer, but the
/// retail field table writes StartCountdownTimer to the member after it, and
/// what the earlier member is for is not established here.
///
/// \par Invariants
/// - Colour indices are consecutive from zero, in the order colours were
///   first defined: a new definition takes the current count as its key.
/// - getColor takes a colour index, or one of two special values that the
///   lobby code uses for player templates: -1 selects the random definition
///   and -2 the observer definition (GameInfo.h in Zero Hour and in the
///   BFME 1 donor).
/// - A definition's tooltip name is what lookups by name compare, and the
///   MultiplayerColor block parser never stores the block's own name token
///   (see INI::parseMultiplayerColorDefinition).
///
/// \par How it is used
/// MultiplayerSettings::getColor is called from game start-up as well as from
/// screens: its callers include the game-logic code that creates a
/// multiplayer game's players, GameSlot::getApparentColor, LANAPI::OnChat
/// and the load-screen and game-setup code. How the lobby sends a player's
/// chosen colour or credit preset to the other machines is not covered here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: how many colours there are and what they look like (one
///   MultiplayerColor block each, in file order), the credit presets, the
///   beacon limit, the shroud switch and the random-choice switches.
/// - Hard-coded: the field names, the five preset names, and the two
///   special indices for random and observer. The random and observer
///   colours cannot be set from these blocks.
/// - There is no override handling: a MultiplayerSettings block read from a
///   map file simply overwrites the values.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The colour list is not a fixed-size array, so code that shows colours
/// has to accept whatever count the data defines. In INI a colour is written
/// as three components from 0 to 255 and stored as real numbers scaled by
/// 1/255; the packed 32-bit form is derived from them when the block ends.
/// \see INI::parseMultiplayerColorDefinition, MultiplayerSettings::getColor

/// \class Image
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_ini_definition_parsers
/// \brief One named rectangle of a texture, called a mapped image, that
///        the GUI, the Apt UI, particle systems and command buttons draw.
///
/// Mapped images are defined in INI by MappedImage blocks. Zero Hour's
/// source refers to an ImagePacker tool: pictures packed onto texture pages
/// can be stored rotated 90 degrees clockwise, and then the size computed
/// from the page rectangle has its width and height exchanged (see
/// Image::parseImageStatus). BFME 2 reads the same fields. Each image is
/// looked up by name through TheMappedImageCollection, for example by
/// GameWindowManager::winFindImage, the player template image getters and
/// AptPlayer::FindRenderImage.
///
/// \par What it holds
/// - A name, which is the MappedImage block's name token, and a texture
///   file name (the Texture field).
/// - The declared size of the texture in texels (TextureWidth and
///   TextureHeight), which the INI states and which is not read from the
///   file.
/// - The image's rectangle in the texture as fractions of that declared
///   size (a pair of corners), and the image's size in texels.
/// - Status flags: ROTATED_90_CLOCKWISE and RAW_TEXTURE are the two names
///   the Status field accepts. Zero Hour: RAW_TEXTURE marks an image that
///   carries raw texture data, which BFME 2's Image also has room for.
/// - An optional pointer to raw texture data.
/// A new image starts with no texture and zero sizes, the full-texture
/// rectangle (0 to 1 in both directions) and no status flags.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// INI::parseMappedImageDefinition allocates an Image with `new` and hands
/// it to the image collection, which keeps a pointer to it in a map whose key
/// is made from the lowercased name. Nothing in the parser frees an image,
/// and lookups return const pointers that callers cache, so treat an image as
/// living as long as the collection. GameClient::init creates the collection
/// and loads it immediately with a texture size of 512, before the animation
/// collection is created from the images.
///
/// \par Invariants
/// - Names are matched without regard to case: both ImageCollection::addImage
///   and ImageCollection::findImageByName turn the name into a key through
///   a lowercasing name-key call.
/// - After Image::parseImageCoords the rectangle holds fractions of the
///   declared texture size, provided that size was nonzero when Coords was
///   read; otherwise the values are still in texels.
/// - Image::parseImageStatus swaps the stored width and height when the
///   rotated flag is set, so it must run after Coords.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: image names, texture file names, declared sizes, the
///   rectangle and the status flags, in MappedImage blocks. ImageCollection::load
///   reads, recursively and in this order, the directories
///   `Data\INI\MappedImages\TextureSize_N` (N is 512 in the only call the
///   code makes), `HandCreated`, `AptImages`, `ParticleTextures` and
///   `TransitionImages`, with the overwrite load type and no CRC writer.
///   Every `.ini` file in them is read, so a mod adds images by adding
///   files there.
/// - A block whose name matches an existing image (ignoring case) updates
///   that image field by field, so a later file changes only the fields it
///   states.
/// - Hard-coded: the five field names, the two status names and the
///   `Left:`, `Top:`, `Right:` and `Bottom:` keys of Coords.
/// - Images are client-side data and are not part of the INI CRC.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Rectangles are stored as fractions of the declared texture size, so a
/// higher-resolution replacement of a texture page with the same layout
/// needs no INI change: leave TextureWidth, TextureHeight and Coords at the
/// original values. The image's size in texels (used for layout) comes from
/// the Coords rectangle itself, not from the file or the declared texture
/// size; changing only the declared texture size changes the stored fractions
/// but not the image size.
/// \see INI::parseMappedImageDefinition, Image::parseImageCoords, Image::parseImageStatus

/// \class CommandButton
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_ini_definition_parsers
/// \brief One definition of a command button: what a button on the command
///        bar does, what it needs and how it looks.
///
/// Command buttons are the entries of command sets (CommandSet), which an
/// object lists to say which buttons its selection shows
/// (\ref page_core_frameworks_commands). A button is more than UI: it names
/// the command type, the object, upgrade, science or special power it uses
/// and the target rules, and both the command bar and game logic read it
/// (for example AIGroup::groupDoCommandButton takes a CommandButton, and
/// CommandButton::isReady decides whether it can be used). Its Command field
/// is read by CommandButton::parseCommand.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// The control bar owns the buttons and looks them up by name.
/// ControlBar::parseCommandButtonDefinition finds a button by name and
/// otherwise creates it. What happens when a name is already defined
/// depends on the load type (\ref sub_ini_flow):
/// - plain overwrite load (the type the legend loader uses): the repeated
///   block is parsed into a throwaway copy, so a bad line is still
///   reported, and then discarded. The first definition wins. Zero Hour
///   reports a duplicate only through a debug-build assertion and then
///   parses the fields into the existing button, so the later block wins.
/// - override load (the type used for map.ini and solo.ini): the existing
///   button is replaced through an override copy, and a button created in
///   this mode is marked as an override.
/// - reload (a load type Zero Hour lacks): a fresh button replaces the old
///   one; what happens to the old one is not covered here
///   (\ref page_core_frameworks_reload).
///
/// \par Fields
/// These are the names in the retail table; this page documents only the
/// Command field.
/// - What the button does and needs: Command, Options, Object, Upgrade,
///   NeededUpgrade, NeededUpgradeAny, BuildUpgrades, Science, SpecialPower,
///   WeaponSlot, WeaponSlotToggle1, WeaponSlotToggle2, WeaponSlotToggle3,
///   FlagsUsedForToggle,
///   MaxShotsToFire, AffectsKindOf, RequireLevel, RequiresValidContainer,
///   CommandRangeStart, CommandRangeCount, Stances, CommandTrigger.
/// - Cursors and toggling: CursorName, InvalidCursorName, RadiusCursorType,
///   ToggleButtonName.
/// - Text and pictures: TextLabel, DescriptLabel, PurchasedLabel,
///   ConflictingLabel, LacksPrerequisiteLabel, ButtonImage, ButtonBorderType.
///   In BFME 2 TextLabel, DescriptLabel and ButtonImage take a list of
///   names, where Zero Hour's take one.
///   ButtonBorderType takes one of NONE, BUILD, UPGRADE, ACTION, SYSTEM or
///   ALTERED.
/// - Sounds: UnitSpecificSound, SetAutoAbilityUnitSound,
///   UnsetAutoAbilityUnitSound.
/// - Flags and auto abilities: DoubleClick, Radial, ShowProductionCount,
///   InPalantir, IsClickable, ShowButton, AutoAbility, AutoDelay,
///   TriggerWhenReady, PresetRange, NeedDamagedTarget,
///   AutoAbilityDisallowedOnModelCondition, EnableOnModelCondition,
///   DisableOnModelCondition.
/// - Create-a-Hero screen: CreateAHeroUIPrerequisiteButtonName,
///   CreateAHeroUIMinimumLevel, CreateAHeroUIIconImageName,
///   CreateAHeroUIAllowableUpgrades.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: everything above, per button, and which buttons a command
///   set shows.
/// - Hard-coded: the field names, the list of command names that Command
///   accepts (a new kind of command needs code on the receiving side), the
///   button border names and the weapon-slot names.
/// - Buttons are read by game logic, so changes to targeting, upgrades or
///   command types are logic changes (\ref page_modding_rules). The reload
///   notice for command data is described on
///   \ref page_core_frameworks_reload.
/// \see CommandButton::parseCommand, ControlBar::parseCommandButtonDefinition,
///      CommandSet::getCommandButton

/// \class W3DModelDrawModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_ini_definition_parsers
/// \brief The shared model draw data: the settings of one Draw line of the
///        W3DScriptedModelDraw family, and the base of the horde, sail, tank
///        and truck draw data.
///
/// BFME 2 registers no module named W3DModelDraw. The name
/// W3DScriptedModelDraw gets this class's data factory, and the horde, sail,
/// tank and truck draw modules build their data on it, so a block of any of
/// them accepts every field below (\ref grp_w1023_w3d_draw_module_factories_more
/// describes the registrations). W3DModelDrawModuleData::buildFieldParse adds
/// the field table that holds them. W3DHordeModelDrawModuleData derives from
/// this class and adds LodOptions.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// The module data factory builds one object per Draw line while a thing
/// template loads. It belongs to the template and is shared by every
/// drawable of it; draw modules only read it (\ref grp_w1021_w3d_draw_module_factories).
///
/// \par Fields
/// The names are those of the retail field table; only the kind of value is
/// stated, and what each field does is not documented here.
/// - States: DefaultModelConditionState and ModelConditionState (read by
///   the condition-state parser), IdleAnimationState, TransitionState and
///   AnimationState (read by an animation-state parser). Each opens a nested
///   block.
/// - Recoil: InitialRecoilSpeed and RecoilSettleSpeed are velocities
///   (INI::parseVelocityReal: per second, stored per logic frame);
///   MaxRecoilDistance and RecoilDamping are plain reals.
/// - Colour and texture: OkToChangeModelColor, UseProducerTexture,
///   TimeOfDayTexture, RandomTexture, RandomTextureFixedRandomIndex,
///   BurntTexture.
/// - Bones and attachments: ExtraPublicBone, AttachToBoneInAnotherModule,
///   AttachModel (W3DScriptedModelDrawModuleData::parseAttachModel),
///   TrackMarks, TrackMarksLeftBone, TrackMarksRightBone,
///   ParticlesAttachedToAnimatedBones, ParticleBonesCheckDrawable,
///   WadingParticleSys, ProjectileBoneFeedbackEnabledSlots (a bit string).
/// - Detail levels: MinLODRequired (a name from a list),
///   HighDetailLODThreshold, LowDetailLODThreshold (reals),
///   SwitchModelLODMode, StaticModelLODMode, HighDetailOnly,
///   MultiPlayerOnly.
/// - Fading and sorting: AlphaRefAnimated, AlphaRefRange (a pair of
///   integers), AlphaCameraFadeOuterRadius and AlphaCameraFadeInnerRadius
///   (positive, nonzero reals), AlphaCameraAtInnerRadius (a percentage
///   stored as a real), StaticSortLevelWhileFading, BirthFadeTime (a
///   duration: milliseconds, stored in logic frames), BirthFadeAdditive,
///   ZWriteDisableOverride.
/// - Shadow and glow: ShadowForceDisable, ShowShadowWhileContained,
///   GlowEnabled, GlowEmissive.
/// - Meshes: RampMesh1, RampMesh2, EmbedPortal, WallBoundsMesh,
///   RaisedWallMesh.
/// - Other switches: NoRotate, UseFiringArcRotation, AnimationsRequirePower,
///   DependencySharedModelFlags, UseStandardModelNames, UseDefaultAnimation,
///   AffectedByStealth.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: all of the fields above, per Draw line, including the
///   model, animation and texture names inside condition states.
/// - Hard-coded: the field names and each module's use of its fields. The
///   fields are not only for drawing: the game logic reads this data to place
///   projectile launch positions, so the models, bones and condition states
///   are logic-visible and must match on every machine in a game
///   (\ref grp_w1023_w3d_draw_module_factories_more, \ref page_modding_rules).
/// - Every Draw line sits in an Object block, so it counts toward the INI
///   data that multiplayer compares (\ref sub_rts_model_modding).
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - MinLODRequired is read as a name from a list of detail levels. Zero Hour:
///   W3DModelDraw's data takes its skip level from MinLODRequired, and the
///   drawable code skips a Draw line whose level is higher than the current
///   static game level. BFME 2 has the same factory skip
///   (\ref grp_w1021_w3d_draw_module_factories) and the same field name, but
///   the link between them was not traced here. A renderer with a different
///   detail policy would need to check how the skip level is obtained before
///   keeping or replacing it. What the other detail fields do is not covered
///   here.
/// - Durations and velocities are converted to the 5 per second logic rate
///   as they are read (\ref grp_w1019_ini_units_name_keys).
/// \note The WorldBuilder twin places the parse callbacks parseConditionState,
///       parseAttachModel and AttachModelStruct::parseModel in
///       W3DScriptedModelDraw.cpp under the class
///       W3DScriptedModelDrawModuleData (checked with tools/wb_show.py).
///       Whether that is the same class under another name or a related
///       class is not established; see W3DScriptedModelDrawModuleData.
/// \see W3DScriptedModelDrawModuleData, W3DModelDrawModuleData::parseLodOptions,
///      W3DHordeModelDrawModuleData

/// \class W3DScriptedModelDrawModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_ini_definition_parsers
/// \brief The class under which the WorldBuilder twin places the model draw
///        parse callbacks that read condition states, attached models and
///        their weights.
///
/// The WorldBuilder twin of the retail executable places the callbacks
/// W3DScriptedModelDrawModuleData::parseConditionState,
/// W3DScriptedModelDrawModuleData::parseAttachModel and
/// W3DScriptedModelDrawModuleData::AttachModelStruct::parseModel in a source
/// file called W3DScriptedModelDraw.cpp. The retail field table that binds
/// them is the one W3DModelDrawModuleData::buildFieldParse registers, and the
/// module registered as W3DScriptedModelDraw takes exactly that table, so in
/// use the callbacks parse the fields of \ref W3DModelDrawModuleData.
/// Whether BFME 2 has two classes or one under two spellings is not
/// established here, and neither is anything about the class beyond these
/// callbacks.
///
/// \par Attached models
/// An AttachModel line adds one record to a list in the module data. A record
/// holds the name of a bone, an offset from that bone, a list of candidate
/// models, each with a probability, and two masks built from the model
/// conditions written on the AttachModel line. The Bone and Offset fields
/// fill the first two; each Model line adds a candidate (see
/// W3DScriptedModelDrawModuleData::parseAttachModel for the syntax and the
/// probability rules). How the draw code picks among the candidates at run
/// time is not covered here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// AttachModel attaches extra models to a bone of the drawn model. The
/// parser does not check the bone or model names, and the probabilities are
/// limited to 100 percent in total.
/// \see W3DModelDrawModuleData, W3DScriptedModelDrawModuleData::parseAttachModel

/// \fn void parseCreateAHeroBlingBinder(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser for Attribute in a Create-a-Hero sub-class: reads one
///        attribute binder (a group name and three upgrade names) and files
///        it with the sub-class.
///
/// A Create-a-Hero class (the CreateAHeroClass block) contains sub-class
/// definitions, read by CreateAHeroManager::parseCreateAHeroSubClass, and a
/// sub-class's field table has an Attribute entry that runs this function.
/// Its siblings include UpgradeName, NameTag, DescriptionTag,
/// SpendableAttributePoints, DefaultFaction and UsableFactions. The
/// function's error messages end "while parsing parseCreateAHeroBlingBinder.".
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The Attribute line carries no value of its own. The function starts a
/// four-part record with group key zero and the name None in each of three
/// string slots, then calls INI::initFromINI on the same reader with a table
/// of four fields, so the lines that follow, up to the matching End, are the
/// binder's fields:
/// | Field | Parsed by | Meaning |
/// |---|---|---|
/// | GroupName | NameKeyGenerator::parseStringAsNameKeyType | the attribute group, kept as a name key |
/// | MinValueUpgrade | INI::parseAsciiString | name of an upgrade |
/// | MaxValueUpgrade | INI::parseAsciiString | name of an upgrade |
/// | DefaultValueUpgrade | INI::parseAsciiString | name of an upgrade |
/// The field names say which upgrade stands for the lowest, the highest and
/// the default value of the attribute; what the sub-class does with the
/// three upgrades afterwards is not established here.
/// The shape, as the parser structure implies it:
/// \code
/// Attribute
///   GroupName = <group>
///   MinValueUpgrade = <upgrade name>
///   MaxValueUpgrade = <upgrade name>
///   DefaultValueUpgrade = <upgrade name>
/// End
/// \endcode
/// After the fields are read the function rejects, with an INIException of
/// code 3, a group key that is zero, that equals the key of the name None or
/// that equals the key of the empty name, and any of the three upgrade names
/// that is None or empty. The two reference keys are looked up once and kept
/// in function-local statics. The messages are, in the order checked:
/// "No group name specified  while parsing parseCreateAHeroBlingBinder." (two
/// spaces, as in retail), then "No minValueUpgradeName specified", "No
/// maxValueUpgradeName specified" and "No defaultValueUpgradeName specified",
/// each followed by " while parsing parseCreateAHeroBlingBinder.". Finally the
/// finished record is handed to the sub-class (the \c instance argument) through
/// one of its member functions, which looks the group key up in a keyed
/// container the sub-class owns and creates an entry when there is none. That
/// is read from the shape of the member's body; its callees are not
/// identified individually, and what happens to the three names when the
/// group key is already present is not established.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the Attribute field name.
/// \param instance The sub-class being filled. It receives the binder.
/// \param store    Not used.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \note A separate block parser, for the CreateAHeroBlingBinder block, also
///       prints messages that end "while parsing parseCreateAHeroBlingBinder."
///       but asks for a label tag, a description tag, a UI slot or a type
///       (fields LabelTag, DescriptionTag, UISlot and BlingType). A message
///       about one of those comes from that block, not from this field.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - All four fields are required. An upgrade name is checked here only for
///   being neither None nor empty, not for being a defined upgrade; a name
///   that matches no upgrade is not reported by this function.
/// - Name keys are compared case-sensitively, so only the exact spelling
///   None is refused as a group name.
/// - Provenance: the field names, the error texts and the order of checks
///   come from the retail body and its tables. The role of the three
///   upgrades is inferred from the field names.
/// \see CreateAHeroManager::parseCreateAHeroSubClass, NameKeyGenerator::parseStringAsNameKeyType,
///      INI::parseAsciiString

/// \fn void parseEmotionNuggetCopyFrom(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser for CopyFrom in an EmotionNugget block: copies
///        another emotion nugget's settings into the nugget being read.
///
/// An emotion nugget (block keyword EmotionNugget) describes one emotion
/// effect that objects can be given, with fields such as Type, Duration,
/// InactiveDuration, StartFXList, UpdateFXList, EndFXList, AttributeModifier,
/// AIState, ModelConditions, PreventPlayerCommands and LuaEvent. Zero Hour
/// has no emotion nuggets. CopyFrom is the first entry of the nugget field
/// table, which makes a nugget a variant of an existing one: write
/// `CopyFrom = <nugget name>` and then the fields that differ.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The function reads one token, looks the name up with
/// EmotionSystem::findNugget and copies the found nugget's settings into the
/// nugget being filled, which is the \c store argument because the CopyFrom
/// field sits at the start of the object. findNugget walks the registered
/// nuggets in registration order, compares each one's name with the token and
/// returns the first match. A nugget is registered when its own block has been
/// read to the end, so a nugget can copy only from one that an earlier block
/// finished. A missing token raises an INIException of code 3 with the text
/// "Name of emotion nugget to copy data from expected."; an unknown name
/// raises code 3 with "Emotion nugget to copy from not found.". The copy is
/// done by the nugget's own copy routine, which leaves two things alone. One
/// is the first member, a string at the start of the object: the block parser
/// assigns the block's name to it before any field is read, so a nugget keeps
/// its own name when it copies another. The other is a tail of two byte-sized
/// members at the end of the object, whose roles are not established here.
/// The copy routine's body copies every member between them.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the CopyFrom field name.
/// \param instance Not used.
/// \param store    [out] The emotion nugget being filled.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - The copy happens when the CopyFrom line is read and, per the copy
///   routine's body, covers every member except the name and the tail
///   described above, so put CopyFrom before the lines that should override
///   it: lines above it are overwritten.
/// - The source nugget has to be defined earlier in load order, in the same
///   file or in a file that loads first. Whether the name comparison ignores
///   case is not established here.
/// - Emotion nuggets feed object behaviour (AIState and PreventPlayerCommands
///   are fields), so they are logic data (\ref page_modding_rules).
/// \see EmotionSystem::findNugget

/// \fn void iniParseEnvironmentSkyboxTextureDefinition(INI *ini)
/// \brief Block parser for SkyboxTextureSet: registers one named set of five
///        skybox texture names.
///
/// A skybox set names the textures of the sky box: SkyboxTextureN,
/// SkyboxTextureE, SkyboxTextureS, SkyboxTextureW and SkyboxTextureT, which
/// by their letters are the four compass sides and the top. The parser lives
/// in the environment INI unit, and GameEngine::init reads `Environment.ini`
/// (a base file in `Data\INI\Default`, then the file of the same name in
/// `Data\INI`) through the CRC writer; which file holds the blocks is data and
/// is not checked here. BFME 1 has the same block. Zero Hour has no
/// SkyboxTextureSet block; its five SkyboxTexture fields (N, E, S, W, T, each
/// INI::parseAsciiString) are part of its water-transparency settings table
/// (Zero Hour: Water.cpp, read by the WaterTransparency block).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The function reads the set's name, then looks it up in the engine's table
/// of sets, which is keyed by name (a hash map in the startup initialiser). The behaviour depends on whether
/// the name is known:
/// - **Known name:** the function returns at once. It does not create a set
///   and does not read the block's field lines. The BFME 1 donor describes a
///   repeated set as a no-op; the loader, however, then reads the field lines
///   as top-level lines and INI::parseLine throws "Unknown block" for the
///   first of them, so, as far as the two bodies show, a repeated set name ends
///   the load with an error instead of being ignored.
/// - **New name:** a set object is allocated with `new` and stored in the
///   table under the name, and the block is read into it with
///   INI::initFromINI. The set starts with the five names TSMorningN.tga,
///   TSMorningE.tga, TSMorningS.tga, TSMorningW.tga and TSMorningT.tga, so
///   a block that leaves a field out keeps that default. The table holds the
///   pointer and nothing here frees it.
/// A set therefore cannot be redefined or overridden by a later block of the
/// same name; a mod that wants different textures uses a new name or edits
/// the original block.
///
/// \param ini The reader, positioned after the SkyboxTextureSet keyword.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Each field is a texture file name (INI::parseAsciiString).
/// - If the blocks sit in Environment.ini, editing it changes the INI CRC,
///   because GameEngine::init reads that file through the CRC writer, although
///   the data only affects drawing (\ref page_modding_rules).
/// - Which code reads the table, and when a map or script selects a set, is
///   not covered here.
/// \see iniParseEnvironmentSkyboxTextureDefinition, INI::parseWaterSettingDefinition

/// \fn static void INI::parseMappedImageDefinition(INI *ini)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_ini_definition_parsers
/// \brief Block parser for MappedImage: finds or creates the named Image in
///        the mapped image collection and reads its fields into it.
///
/// This is the parser behind every entry of the `MappedImages` data: one
/// block per image, giving its texture page, the declared page size, the
/// rectangle and optional status flags. It is Zero Hour's parser, and the
/// field table is Zero Hour's too. \ref Image describes the object and where
/// ImageCollection::load looks for the files.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// 1. Read the image name. If TheMappedImageCollection does not exist, return
///    without reading the block (it exists once GameClient::init has run).
/// 2. Look the name up with ImageCollection::findImageByName. The key is built
///    from the lowercased name, so the match ignores case.
/// 3. If there is no such image, allocate one with `new`, give it the name
///    exactly as written and add it to the collection.
/// 4. Read the block's fields into the image with INI::initFromINI. The table
///    is Texture (INI::parseAsciiString), TextureWidth and TextureHeight
///    (INI::parseInt), Coords (Image::parseImageCoords) and Status
///    (Image::parseImageStatus).
/// There is no load-type test and no reset: a block for an existing image
/// changes only the fields it states.
///
/// \param ini The reader, positioned after the MappedImage keyword.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Write TextureWidth and TextureHeight before Coords, and Status after
///   Coords (see the field parsers).
/// - Names are case-insensitive for lookup, so two blocks that differ only
///   in case describe one image; the first spelling is the one stored.
/// - A repeated block patches the earlier image field by field. Files load in
///   the directory order that ImageCollection::load uses, so a later directory
///   can change an image from an earlier one.
/// \see Image::parseImageCoords, Image::parseImageStatus, ImageCollection::findImageByName,
///      ImageCollection::addImage, ImageCollection::load

/// \fn static void INI::parseMultiplayerColorDefinition(INI *ini)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_ini_definition_parsers
/// \brief Block parser for MultiplayerColor: finds or creates a colour in
///        the multiplayer colour list, reads its fields and packs its colours.
///
/// These blocks build the colour list that MultiplayerSettings indexes (see
/// \ref MultiplayerSettings). Zero Hour's block has the fields TooltipName, RGBColor and
/// RGBNightColor; BFME 2 adds LivingWorldColor, LivingWorldBannerColor and
/// AvailableInWotR (how the Living World uses them is outside this page).
/// RGBColor, RGBNightColor, LivingWorldColor and LivingWorldBannerColor are
/// read by INI::parseRGBColor, which expects the form
/// `R:<r> G:<g> B:<b>`, rejects a component outside 0 to 255 with the message
/// "color value %s=%i out of range (0..255)" and stores each component
/// multiplied by 1/255 as a real. AvailableInWotR is read by INI::parseBool
/// and TooltipName by INI::parseAsciiString.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The function reads the block's name token and calls
///   MultiplayerSettings::findMultiplayerColorDefinitionByName with it. That
///   search compares the token with each existing colour's tooltip name, in
///   index order, not with any name of the block itself.
/// - If nothing matches, MultiplayerSettings::newMultiplayerColorDefinition
///   appends a colour at the next index. The name argument of that function is
///   not stored (Zero Hour's body is the same), so a new colour's tooltip name
///   is whatever its default constructor sets until the TooltipName field
///   runs.
/// - The fields are read into the colour, and then the function packs the
///   colour into its 32-bit form: setColor with the RGBColor value, then
///   setNightColor with the RGBNightColor value. The Living World colours
///   are not packed.
///
/// \par Consequences, from those bodies
/// - A block's name token only decides whether it updates an existing colour,
///   and then only if that colour's TooltipName equals the token. If the
///   TooltipName differs from the block name, a second block with the same
///   name does not find the first and appends another colour.
/// - Because colours are appended in the order of the blocks that create them,
///   the order of definition decides the colour indices.
///
/// \param ini The reader, positioned after the MultiplayerColor keyword.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Give each MultiplayerColor block the same name as its TooltipName if it
///   should be redefinable by later files.
/// - There is no limit in these functions on the number of colours.
/// - Colour indices come from definition order. Code that stores a colour as
///   an index would see colours shift if blocks were reordered or removed;
///   this page does not cover which code does.
/// \see MultiplayerSettings, MultiplayerSettings::getColor, INI::parseRGBColor

/// \fn static void INI::parseTerrainRoadDefinition(INI *ini)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_ini_definition_parsers
/// \brief Block parser for Road: finds or creates a road type in the terrain
///        road collection and reads its fields.
///
/// A road block names a road and gives the texture used to draw it and two
/// widths: Texture (INI::parseAsciiString), RoadWidth and RoadWidthInTexture
/// (both INI::parseReal). Zero Hour has the same parser and the same three
/// fields. Roads and bridges live in one collection, TheTerrainRoads, which
/// has a separate lookup for each (Zero Hour keeps separate lists), and
/// Bridge blocks have their own parser (INI::parseTerrainBridgeDefinition)
/// with many more fields.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The function reads the road's name and asks TheTerrainRoads for a road of
/// that name. If one exists and its bridge flag is set, the load stops with an
/// INIException of code 3, "Redefining bridge '%s' as a road!". If none
/// exists, TerrainRoadCollection::newRoad creates one. The block's fields are
/// then read into the road, so a repeated Road block changes only the fields
/// it states. There is no load-type test. The bridge parser is the mirror
/// image and refuses a road with "Redefining road '%s' as a bridge!". In Zero
/// Hour the road and bridge lookups search separate lists, and BFME 2 calls
/// the same two lookups, so the two errors look like safeguards that normal
/// data does not reach: a road and a bridge that share a name would each be
/// found only by their own block.
///
/// \param ini The reader, positioned after the Road keyword.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - TerrainRoadCollection::newRoad looks for a road named DefaultRoad and,
///   if there is one, copies its texture and its two widths into the new road
///   (Zero Hour does the same). Define DefaultRoad first to give every road a
///   baseline; what a new road holds when there is no DefaultRoad is not
///   covered here.
/// - The road collection is a subsystem that GameEngine::init registers;
///   where its files are listed is decided by the subsystem legend.
/// \see INI::parseTerrainBridgeDefinition, TerrainRoadCollection::newRoad

/// \fn void iniParseFactionVictoryDefinition(INI *ini)
/// \brief Block parser for FactionVictoryData: finds or creates the victory
///        parameters of one faction and reads their five values.
///
/// Zero Hour has no victory system. In the BFME 1 donor sources it is a
/// game-logic subsystem tied to a cell grid over the map, and BFME 2 registers
/// a subsystem under the name TheVictorySystem in GameEngine::init and saves
/// it as a game-state chunk. This block gives each faction its own numbers;
/// iniParseVictorySystemDefinition sets the system-wide ones.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// 1. Read the block's name. A name equal to "neutral", compared without regard
///    to case, becomes the empty name, so the record for no faction is written
///    under the empty name.
/// 2. If TheVictorySystem does not exist, write the debug report "TheVictorySystem
///    has not been initialized!." and return without reading the block.
/// 3. Ask the victory system for the parameters of that name. The system's
///    lookup is described by the report text of the failure case as find or
///    create; if it returns nothing, write the debug report "Could not
///    find/create FactionVictoryParameters in
///    iniParseFactionVictoryDefinition!." and return without reading the block.
/// 4. Read the fields into the record with INI::initFromINI: AllyDeathScaleFactor,
///    EnemyKillScaleFactor, VictoryThreshold, MapToCellVictoryRatio and
///    MajorUnitValue, all read by INI::parseReal and stored as written.
///
/// \param ini The reader, positioned after the FactionVictoryData keyword.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Use the faction's name for a faction block and Neutral (any case) for the
///   neutral one. Whether other names are matched without regard to case is
///   not established here.
/// - Treat the values as logic data: in the BFME 1 donor sources the victory
///   system is updated from the game logic, and BFME 2 saves it with the game
///   state, so a mod that changes them must change them on every machine
///   (\ref page_modding_rules).
/// - What each value means is not documented here.
/// \see iniParseVictorySystemDefinition

/// \fn void iniParseVictorySystemDefinition(INI *ini)
/// \brief Block parser for VictorySystemData: reads the victory system's four
///        tuning values.
///
/// There is one victory system, so the block selects nothing by name: the
/// function reads a name token after the keyword and does not use it other
/// than as an unused argument of the debug report. Zero Hour has no victory
/// system (see iniParseFactionVictoryDefinition for what is known about it).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// If TheVictorySystem does not exist, the function writes the debug report
/// "TheVictorySystem has not been initialized!." and returns without reading
/// the block. Otherwise it reads the block into the system with
/// INI::initFromINI. The fields are CellSize, ScalePerLogicFrame,
/// SubtractPerLogicFrame and CellBonusRadius, all read by INI::parseReal and
/// stored as written. The two PerLogicFrame values are therefore taken to be
/// per logic frame already: unlike a velocity or duration field there is no
/// conversion from seconds or milliseconds. There is no load-type test, so a
/// later block overwrites only the fields it states.
///
/// \param ini The reader, positioned after the VictorySystemData keyword.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Logic data, saved with the game state; keep it identical on all machines
///   (\ref page_modding_rules).
/// - Because the PerLogicFrame values are stored unscaled, they would not
///   follow a change of the logic rate (\ref page_frame_loop).
/// \see iniParseFactionVictoryDefinition

/// \fn static void INI::parseWaterSettingDefinition(INI *ini)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_ini_definition_parsers
/// \brief Block parser for WaterSet: selects the water settings of one time
///        of day by name and reads their fields.
///
/// Water is configured once per time of day. The engine keeps an array of
/// water settings with one slot for each entry of its time-of-day name
/// table, and a WaterSet block fills the slot that its name selects. The
/// water renderer loads the sky and water textures named in the slot for the
/// current time of day. The block is the same as Zero Hour's, apart from the
/// time-of-day list.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The function reads one token and compares it, without regard to case,
/// with the time-of-day names in order. The table holds NONE, MORNING,
/// AFTERNOON, EVENING, NIGHT and INTERPOLATE; BFME 2 appends INTERPOLATE to
/// Zero Hour's list. The position of the first match picks the slot. When no
/// name matches, the load stops with an INIException of code 3, "No water
/// setting found for time of day '%s'". Otherwise the block is read into the
/// slot with INI::initFromINI. The fields are:
/// | Field | Parsed by |
/// |---|---|
/// | SkyTexture, WaterTexture | INI::parseAsciiString (texture file names) |
/// | Vertex00Color, Vertex10Color, Vertex01Color, Vertex11Color | INI::parseRGBAColorInt (see below) |
/// | DiffuseColor, TransparentDiffuseColor | INI::parseRGBAColorInt (see below) |
/// | UScrollPerMS, VScrollPerMS, SkyTexelsPerUnit | INI::parseReal |
/// | WaterRepeatCount | INI::parseInt |
/// A colour is written `R:<r> G:<g> B:<b> A:<a>`; each component must lie in
/// 0 to 255, and A may be left out (it then reads 255). The four whole
/// numbers are stored as they are. The scroll values are stored as written,
/// with no conversion to logic frames (the field names end in "PerMS").
/// Zero Hour: WaterRenderObjClass::renderSky multiplies them by the
/// milliseconds elapsed since its previous call, and the BFME 1 donor does
/// the same; the BFME 2 sky-drawing code was not checked here. The function
/// has no load-type test. The donor comment (Zero Hour and
/// BFME 1) says the block does not support overrides, and nothing here
/// remembers the previous values, so, as far as this parser goes, a WaterSet
/// block in a map file overwrites the shared setting and nothing here
/// restores it.
///
/// \param ini The reader, positioned after the WaterSet keyword.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Use one of the six names; the list is hard-coded. A block for a name
///   that is not in the table ends the load.
/// - GameEngine::init reads `Water.ini`, the base file first and then the
///   file in `Data\INI`, through the CRC writer (\ref page_modding_rules); which
///   file holds the WaterSet blocks is data and is not checked here.
/// - The two texture fields are file names; no check at parse time that the
///   file exists.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The parser stores the scroll values without any frame conversion. If the
/// BFME 2 sky update consumes them per elapsed millisecond as Zero Hour's
/// does (not checked here), they would keep their pace when the logic rate
/// changes; check the BFME 2 consumer before relying on that. Colours are
/// four 8-bit components held as integers, so a renderer with wider colour
/// formats has to convert them.
/// \see iniParseEnvironmentSkyboxTextureDefinition

/// \fn static void INI::parseMiscEvaData(INI *ini)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_ini_definition_parsers
/// \brief Block parser for MiscEvaData: reads the Eva announcer's global
///        settings into the Eva object.
///
/// Eva is the in-game announcer system. Besides one definition per event it
/// has a single bundle of general settings, which this block fills. Zero Hour
/// has no such block; it first appears in BFME 1, whose donor source notes
/// that the bundle sits inside the Eva object itself rather than in a
/// registry. BFME 2 does the same: the function reads the block straight into
/// TheEva. Eva reads `Eva.ini` from its own initialisation, which bypasses
/// the subsystem legend and the CRC writer.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// If the load type is the override type (the one map.ini and solo.ini use),
/// the function throws an INIException of code 8, "Cannot override MiscEvaData
/// entries". Otherwise it reads the block into TheEva with INI::initFromINI.
/// The block takes no name. The fields, from the retail table:
/// | Field | Parsed by | Stored as |
/// |---|---|---|
/// | EnemySightedMaxVoicePositionScanRange | INI::parseReal | a real, as written |
/// | EnemyCampDestroyedDamageTimeoutMS | INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt | logic frames, rounded up |
/// | FriendlyCampDestroyedDamageTimeoutMS | INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt | logic frames, rounded up |
/// | MaxMillisecondsToKeepJumpToEvents | INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt | logic frames, rounded up |
/// | MaxMillisecondsBeforeResettingLastJumpTo | INI::parseUnsignedInt | an unsigned integer, as written |
/// | MinDistanceBetweenJumpToEvents | INI::parseReal | a real, as written |
/// Three fields are durations given in milliseconds and stored in logic
/// frames (at 5 frames per second a value becomes a multiple of 200
/// milliseconds), while MaxMillisecondsBeforeResettingLastJumpTo keeps the
/// number as written even though its name also ends in milliseconds. What the
/// code that reads them compares them with is not covered here, and the
/// defaults that Eva's constructor sets are not stated.
///
/// \param ini The reader, positioned after the MiscEvaData keyword.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Keep MiscEvaData out of map.ini and solo.ini, which load in the override
///   mode that this block refuses.
/// - Remember the unit difference between the three duration fields and the
///   one unsigned field when scaling them.
/// \see INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt

/// \fn MultiplayerColorDefinition *MultiplayerSettings::getColor(Int which)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_ini_definition_parsers
/// \brief Returns the colour definition for a colour index or for the
///        random or observer choice, or null.
///
/// This is the accessor through which game start-up, the lobby and the
/// screens read a colour definition; \ref MultiplayerSettings lists some of
/// its callers.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - -1 (the random player template) returns the random definition and -2
///   (the observer template) returns the observer definition, whatever the
///   list holds.
/// - Any other negative value returns null.
/// - Otherwise the cached colour count is refilled from the size of the colour
///   map when it reads zero, and an index below the count returns the map
///   entry for that index. An index at or above the count returns null.
/// The function returns a pointer to the stored definition, not a copy.
/// Zero Hour calls getNumColors and indexes the map directly; BFME 2's body
/// has the refill written inline and goes through a helper that takes the
/// key by address (BFME 1's shape), and it is not established whether that
/// helper would insert an entry for an unknown key. Within the count it never
/// has to, because indices are consecutive.
///
/// \param which A colour index from zero, or -1 for random or -2 for observer.
/// \return The colour definition, or null for an index that is out of range.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The number of valid indices is the number of MultiplayerColor blocks that
/// created a colour (see INI::parseMultiplayerColorDefinition). The random
/// and observer definitions are never read from INI blocks.
/// \see MultiplayerSettings, INI::parseMultiplayerColorDefinition

/// \fn static void CommandButton::parseCommand(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_ini_definition_parsers
/// \brief Field parser for Command in a CommandButton block: turns a command
///        name into the command type number.
///
/// Every button has a command type that says what it does when clicked (build
/// a unit, cast a special power, guard and so on). INI states it as a name
/// and this parser stores the name's position in the engine's command-name
/// table. The control bar tests that number against its own constants, so the
/// table order is the numbering: the control bar and multi-select code
/// compare the stored value with literal numbers (for example 25, the
/// position of PURCHASE_SCIENCE, and 10, the position of ATTACK_MOVE).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The function reads one token with INI::getNextToken and walks the
/// null-terminated table from the first entry, comparing each name with the
/// token without regard to case (the C runtime's case-insensitive string
/// compare, imported). The first match stores its position through \c store as
/// an integer and returns. A token with no match throws an INIException of
/// code 3, "Command '%s' not found". Zero Hour does the same walk over
/// TheGuiCommandNames and throws an error code without a message.
///
/// The table holds these names, in this order: NONE, FOUNDATION_CONSTRUCT,
/// FOUNDATION_CONSTRUCT_CANCEL, UNIT_BUILD, SECONDARY_UNIT_BUILD,
/// CANCEL_UNIT_BUILD, PLAYER_UPGRADE, OBJECT_UPGRADE, CASTLE_UPGRADE,
/// CANCEL_UPGRADE, ATTACK_MOVE, GUARD, GUARD_WITHOUT_PURSUIT,
/// GUARD_FLYING_UNITS_ONLY, STOP, WAYPOINTS, EXIT_CONTAINER, EVACUATE,
/// EVACUATE_CONTESTED, EXECUTE_UNUSED_01, BEACON_DELETE, SET_RALLY_POINT,
/// SELL, FIRE_WEAPON, SPECIAL_POWER, PURCHASE_SCIENCE, COMBATDROP,
/// SWITCH_WEAPON, AUTOCAST_WEAPON, HIJACK_VEHICLE, CONVERT_TO_CARBOMB,
/// PLACE_BEACON, SPECIAL_POWER_FROM_COMMAND_CENTER, CASTLE_UNPACK,
/// CASTLE_PACK, TOGGLE_WEAPON, HORDE_TOGGLE_FORMATION, SPECIAL_POWER_TOGGLE,
/// SPELL_BOOK, ONE_RING, CREW_EVACUATE, BLOODTHIRSTY, CLOSE_GATE, OPEN_GATE,
/// TOGGLE_GATE, TOGGLE_WEAPONSET, REVIVE, MONSTERDOCK,
/// TOGGLE_NO_AUTO_ACQUIRE, WAKE_AUTO_PICKUP, CASTLE_UNPACK_EXPLICIT_OBJECT,
/// START_SELF_REPAIR, HORDE_SET_FORMATION, DOZER_CONSTRUCT,
/// DOZER_CONSTRUCT_CANCEL, PUSH_VISIBLE_COMMAND_RANGE,
/// POP_VISIBLE_COMMAND_RANGE, TOGGLE_STANCE, SET_STANCE,
/// START_NEIGHBORHOOD_REPAIR and CANCEL_NEIGHBORHOOD. That is the whole
/// table. What each command does is up to the code that receives the
/// number and is not described here.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the Command field name.
/// \param instance Not used.
/// \param store    [out] The integer field that receives the command type.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Command accepts only the names above, in any letter case. A new kind of
///   command needs code.
/// - The stored value is the table position, and other code compares it with
///   literal numbers, so the order of the table cannot change without changing
///   that code.
/// \see CommandButton, ControlBar::parseCommandButtonDefinition

/// \fn static void Image::parseImageCoords(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_ini_definition_parsers
/// \brief Field parser for Coords in a MappedImage block: reads the image's
///        rectangle in the texture and stores it as texture fractions.
///
/// The value has the form `Coords = Left:10 Top:20 Right:74 Bottom:84`: four
/// numbers, each with the key written before a colon, in that order. The
/// function turns the rectangle into the image's normalised rectangle (what
/// the renderer samples) and its size in texels.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// 1. Read the four values with INI::getNextSubToken, which expects the named
///    key before the colon, and INI::scanInt, which also accepts the hash
///    math expressions described on \ref sub_ini_pieces.
/// 2. Build the rectangle from the corner (Left, Top) and the corner
///    (Right - 1, Bottom - 1) as real numbers: BFME pulls the far edges in
///    by one texel. Zero Hour uses Right and Bottom unchanged.
/// 3. Scale by the image's declared texture size. For each axis whose declared
///    size is not zero, both coordinates are multiplied by the reciprocal of
///    the size, where Zero Hour divides. A declared size of zero leaves that
///    axis in texels, with no error.
/// 4. Store the rectangle in the image, and store the image size as
///    (Right - Left, Bottom - Top), without the one-texel adjustment.
/// The function writes through \c instance, the Image; the \c store argument
/// is not used.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the Coords field name.
/// \param instance The Image being filled.
/// \param store    Not used.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - TextureWidth and TextureHeight must come before Coords. They are read
///   when Coords runs; if they are still zero the rectangle stays in texels.
/// - The declared size is only a scale: it does not have to match the
///   file, and the image's size in texels is the INI rectangle's size.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Because the stored rectangle is a fraction, a texture page replaced by a
/// larger one with the same layout needs no INI change as long as the
/// declared size and coordinates keep the original values. The one-texel
/// inset is taken in declared texels before scaling, so it is the same
/// fraction of the page whatever the file's real resolution; on a
/// higher-resolution page it spans proportionally more file pixels.
/// \see Image::parseImageStatus, INI::parseMappedImageDefinition

/// \fn static void Image::parseImageStatus(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_ini_definition_parsers
/// \brief Field parser for Status in a MappedImage block: reads the image's
///        status flags and swaps its width and height for a rotated image.
///
/// The value is a list of flag names. Two names exist: ROTATED_90_CLOCKWISE
/// and RAW_TEXTURE (Zero Hour: the second marks an image whose structure
/// carries raw texture data). The status word is the stored field.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The function calls INI::parseBitString32 with the two-name list, which
/// sets the bits for the names given. Then, if the rotated bit is set in the
/// result, it exchanges the image's stored width and height. The sizes were
/// computed from the rectangle on the texture page, which for a rotated
/// image is turned a quarter, so the swap restores the size that the image
/// has when drawn upright (Zero Hour's comment, citing the ImagePacker tool).
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the Status field name.
/// \param instance The Image being filled.
/// \param store    [out] The status word.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Put Status after Coords. The swap acts on the size that Coords has
///   already stored; if Status comes first, the swap runs on the starting
///   size of zero and the later Coords leaves the size unswapped.
/// \see Image::parseImageCoords

/// \fn static void W3DModelDrawModuleData::parseLodOptions(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_ini_definition_parsers
/// \brief Field parser for LodOptions in a W3DHordeModelDraw block: selects
///        one of three detail levels and reads that level's options.
///
/// W3DHordeModelDrawModuleData holds three sets of options, one for each of
/// LOW, MEDIUM and HIGH detail. A LodOptions line names the level and opens a
/// nested field list that ends at its own End line:
/// \code
/// LodOptions = LOW
///   AllowMultipleModels = No
///   MaxRandomTextures = 1
/// End
/// \endcode
/// W3DHordeModelDraw's own field table registers it
/// (W3DHordeModelDrawModuleData::buildFieldParse adds that table, then the
/// shared model draw table), and the three option sets are part of the horde
/// module data. This page has not checked whether another module's table
/// also registers it. The parser follows the BFME 1 donor, which spells it as
/// a member of W3DModelDrawModuleData; the evidence that its option records
/// belong to the horde data is the horde module data constructor, which
/// initialises three option records.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The function reads one token, converts it to lower case and compares it
/// with low, medium and high. Case does not matter. Anything else throws an
/// INIException of code 1, "Expected LOW, MEDIUM, or HIGH". The index
/// 0, 1 or 2 selects one of three option records in the module data, and the
/// function reads the nested fields into that record with INI::initFromINI.
/// Each record holds the same five options:
/// | Field | Parsed by |
/// |---|---|
/// | AllowMultipleModels | INI::parseBool |
/// | MaxRandomTextures, MaxRandomAnimations, RandomStartFramePercent | INI::parseInt |
/// | MaxAnimFrameDelta | INI::parseReal |
/// Each level can be given once or several times, and a level never written
/// keeps the constructor's values. The constructor of
/// W3DHordeModelDrawModuleData sets:
/// | Level | AllowMultipleModels | MaxRandomTextures | MaxRandomAnimations | MaxAnimFrameDelta | RandomStartFramePercent |
/// |---|---|---|---|---|---|
/// | LOW | No | 1 | 1 | 15.0 | 0 |
/// | MEDIUM | Yes | 2 | 2 | 8.0 | 50 |
/// | HIGH | Yes | 999 | 999 | 0.5 | 100 |
/// What the options do to the horde's drawing, and how the draw module
/// chooses a level, are not covered here.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the LodOptions field name.
/// \param instance The module data being filled. The option records are part
///                 of the horde module data, so it must be one.
/// \param store    Not used.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Specify each level you want to change; unspecified levels keep the
///   defaults above.
/// - The names LOW, MEDIUM and HIGH are the only levels. A fourth cannot be
///   added without code.
/// \see W3DHordeModelDraw, W3DHordeModelDrawModuleData

/// \fn static void W3DScriptedModelDrawModuleData::parseAttachModel(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_ini_definition_parsers
/// \brief Field parser for AttachModel in a model draw block: reads one
///        attached-model definition, checks and completes its probabilities
///        and appends it to the module data.
///
/// An AttachModel entry puts one of several candidate models on a bone.
/// The line may be followed on the same line by model condition names, then
/// a nested field list that ends at its own End line:
/// \code
/// AttachModel = <condition> NOT_<condition>
///   Bone = <bone name>
///   Offset = X:0 Y:0 Z:0
///   Model = <model name> Prob:60
///   Model = <model name>
/// End
/// \endcode
/// (The shape follows the parsers. The conditions are optional.)
///
/// \par Implementation
/// 1. If \c instance is null, return.
/// 2. Start a record with a zero offset. Read the condition names left on the
///    line with the model-condition-flags reader, which fills two masks: one
///    marks every condition named, the other records for each whether it was
///    written plainly or with a NOT_ prefix. Then read the nested fields with
///    INI::initFromINI: Bone (INI::parseAsciiString), Offset
///    (INI::parseCoord3D) and Model (W3DScriptedModelDrawModuleData::AttachModelStruct::parseModel).
/// 3. Each Model line adds a candidate: a model name, then an optional Prob
///    value from 1 to 100 (separated from the key by a space or a colon). A
///    candidate without Prob is marked unassigned. A Prob outside 1 to 100
///    throws "probability must be between 1 and 100".
/// 4. Validate, with INIException code 3 and the prefix "*** ASSET ERROR:", in
///    this order: an empty bone name ("you must specify then bone name" - the
///    word "then" is in retail's text); no candidate ("you must specify at
///    least one model name"); assigned probabilities that total more than 100
///    ("combined probability may not be higher than 100"); assigned
///    probabilities plus the number of unassigned candidates above 100
///    ("can't auto-assign probabilities"; each unassigned candidate needs at
///    least 1).
/// 5. Share what is left of 100 among the unassigned candidates: each gets the
///    remainder divided by their count, rounded down, and the last takes
///    what is left.
/// 6. A final check throws "combined probability must be 100" if the total
///    is above 100. After the checks above it cannot fire. Because the test
///    is for above 100 and not for not equal to 100, a record whose
///    candidates all have a Prob that adds up to less than 100 is accepted
///    as it is.
/// 7. Append the record to the list in the module data.
/// No model or bone name is checked against assets while parsing.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the AttachModel field name.
/// \param instance The module data being filled. It receives the record.
/// \param store    Not used.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Give a bone name and at least one Model. Probabilities are whole
///   percentages; leave Prob out on some candidates to split the rest evenly.
/// - Candidates written with Prob must total at most 100, and leaving
///   Prob off several candidates needs at least 1 percent for each.
/// - How the draw code picks among the candidates, and what happens when the
///   totals are below 100, is not covered here.
/// \see W3DScriptedModelDrawModuleData, W3DModelDrawModuleData

